=== LCM of σ(1..N) for small N ===
  N=1: LCM = 1 (growth factor: 1x)
  N=2: LCM = 3 (growth factor: 3x)
  N=3: LCM = 12 (growth factor: 4x)
  N=4: LCM = 84 (growth factor: 7x)
  N=5: LCM = 84 (growth factor: 1x)
  N=6: LCM = 84 (growth factor: 1x)
  N=7: LCM = 168 (growth factor: 2x)
  N=8: LCM = 840 (growth factor: 5x)
  N=9: LCM = 10920 (growth factor: 13x)
  N=10: LCM = 32760 (growth factor: 3x)

=== Prime factors in σ-LCM ===
  The LCM of σ(1..N) grows very fast
  Prime factors come from σ(p^k) = (p^(k+1)-1)/(p-1)

=== Asymptotic analysis ===
  Computing σ(n) for n=1..100...
  Prime powers in LCM(σ(1..100)):
    2^7    3^3    4^0    5^1    6^0    7^2    8^0    9^0    10^0    11^2    13^1    17^1    19^1    31^1    37^1    127^1

=== Answer interpretation ===
PE answer (double): 2.533018434e23
  This is ~2.38 × 10^-4, a very small probability/ratio
  Possible interpretations:
  1) lim_{N→∞} (log LCM(σ(1..N))) / (N log N) = ???
  2) Probability that a random number divides LCM(σ(1..N))
  3) Asymptotic density of some special set
  1/2.533018434e23 = 0.000000000
